                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Writes a list or 2-D array to a text file, one row per line, values separated by commas (or tabs or spaces). A spreadsheet, a text editor, R, Excel or any other program can open it; np.loadtxt reads it back.\n\nText is easy to share and to read by eye, but it is larger than np.save's format, keeps only 1-D and 2-D arrays, and writes each value to 9 significant digits. Without an extension, .csv is added.\n\nUse it to hand measurements to other software, or to look at numbers a patch has produced.\n\nWRITING ONLY WHEN ASKED:\nAn array arriving at in is only held, not written: a stream of arrays does not rewrite the file sixty times a second. The file is written when write is clicked (or sent a bang).\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.savetxt <path>\nDefault: no path - the first write asks where to save.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.savetxt recordings/take_1.csv\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array to save (a list is turned into one). Held until written; each new one replaces the last.\n\nwrite (button, also an inlet):\nWrites the held array to the path. With no path yet, it asks where, as save as does.\n\nsave as (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file name and folder, writes there, and keeps that path for the next write.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: where write saves. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from. It is saved with the patch.\n\ndelimiter:\nAn option, under the node: comma (the default), tab or space between the values on a line.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsaved path:\nThe full path of the file, sent each time it is written - ready to pass on, or to show where the file went.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA 2 x 3 table arrive and are held. Click write (on the node) and choose where to save; the path appears below. Nothing is saved until you do, and nothing is saved in the help folder.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.loadtxt reads the file back. np.save writes the other format."
        },
